00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
As mentioned earlier, in order for miners to earn block rewards, they have to verify the authenticity of Bitcoin transactions

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.680
For example, If I myself send 5 bitcoins to another person

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.480
what is the way for that person to guarantee that I have not sent those same coins

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:20.000
to a third party or many others since it's digital currency and just codes?

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.120
Miners are the ones who do the job. They process the transactions and verify their authenticity

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:30.400
Then, they piece them together in a block and include it to the blockchain.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.400
Herewith, their reward is recorded at the top of the block itself.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.640
Here we come to the questions of today's episode, the first of which is;

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.440
who is the first miner of Bitcoin? Was he/she mining Bitcoin on his own?

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.160
How many blocks had he/she mined? How many bitcoins does he/she have?

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.200
Is he/she still mining or they stopped a long time ago?

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:57.400
 I remind you that this series is educational episodes directed to viewers in countries that allow cryptocurrency trading

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.040
This is what we will be answering and discussing in today's episode.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:05.560
I am Ashraf Ibrahim, and this is our seventh episode of our new series on cryptocurrencies, "Crypto Economic Informant".

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.560
Assume that Satoshi Nakamoto, the inventor of Bitcoin, is one person,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.800
he is the first person to run a mining computer in January 2009

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:35.320
Thus, he had the ability to mine many blocks and extract a large number of bitcoins.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:41.880
No one at that time was interested in the subject because Bitcoin itself had no value at all.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:47.200
There weren't many miners back then. Therefore, mining was not as difficult as it is now.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:53.040
Only a few people were dedicating their home computers to mining Bitcoin

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.080
and they were doing it out of exploration and curiosity because Bitcoin itself had no value.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:05.280
Satoshi Nakimoto himself used a regular computer with an average CPU unit to mine the first blocks back then.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:11.280
Now, it is quite complicated because of the difficulty of mining itself. This will be explained in detail later, God willing.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.160
As previously mentioned, Satoshi was given 50 bitcoins as a block reward for each block he mined.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:22.000
Every 10 minutes there is a new block that would come to existence and be included in the blockchain.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:29.200
Of course, with the exception of the second block, which is No. 1, wherein he took 6 days to mine as aforementioned.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.800
A question arises: How is the No. 1 block the second one? That is a really good question.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.400
Because in programming, numbers start from zero,

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:45.000
so the formation block was number 0 and the second block number 1.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.680
So how many blocks did Satoshi mine?

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:53.880
In fact, no one knows for sure how many blocks because people are not known in the blockchain as explained before

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:59.400
Just addresses such as the email address are numbers and letters only, it is not possible to know its owner.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.720
However, there are studies and investigations that traced the entity that made the formation block,

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:11.400
which is of course Satoshi Nakamoto himself. Among the controversial theories is the study of Sergio Demian Lerner,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.800
a researcher in the field of security and cryptocurrency, entitled:

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:20.200
“The Well Deserved Fortune of Satoshi Nakamoto, Bitcoin creator, Visionary and Genius.”

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:28.360
Lerner's study tracked blocks that were mined between January 1, 2009 and January 25, 2010 -

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:32.440
blocks 1 through 36288 -

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.760
to identify blocks that have been mined by the same entity

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:43.560
When he did that, he found that there was one entity using one mining rig and mining thousands of blocks in this period.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:50.840
Lerner calls the pattern of blocks that are mined from the same individual entity the Patoshi Pattern,

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:56.600
and it is assumed that this Patoshi is Satoshi Nakamoto because this pattern begins with the formation block.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:01.480
Lerner found that Bateushi mined nearly 22,000 blocks on his own

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.520
and earned a total of 1,100,000 bitcoins as a block reward.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:13.320
Thus, this makes him the most productive miner in the history of Bitcoin and the most proprietary of its currencies.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:18.000
Recent research suggests that Satoshi could have mined more bitcoins afterwards.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:26.600
But he stopped on purpose to give other miners a fair chance to mine and so they too could get bitcoin.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:33.600
This means that Satoshi was mining Bitcoin for the first few months in 2009, after which he stopped completely.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.240
Of course, the value of one million and one hundred thousand bitcoins in 2009 was almost zero.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.680
However, if we calculate the net wealth of Satoshi Nakamoto

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.600
at the current price of bitcoin at the highest peak in the first half of 2022,

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:54.400
which is about 47 thousand dollars. So, One million and one thousand bitcoins make a fortune exceeding 51 billion dollars,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:06.200
and this puts him in the list of the world's richest people in the Bloomberg Billionaires Index of the world.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.000
Although he owns a bitcoin worth more than 51 billion dollars,

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.160
there is no ground evidence to date saying that Satoshi transferred,

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.040
sold or disposed of any number of these currencies.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:27.280
According to the pattern of Batoshi, the approximately one million bitcoins that Satoshi owns are still in their places since 2009.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.800
Any currencies ever created in the first months of 2009, if

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.440
transferred from one wallet to another, would be monitored.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.440
This move, due to its importance, is considered to attract the attention of the entire financial Sector.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:45.400
They will then check whether these altcoins follow the patoshi pattern and therefore are for satoshi himself or not.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:51.480
As happened on May 20, 2020, when 40 bitcoins were transferred from an inactive account

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:58.400
its bitcoins were extracted on February 9, 2009 - in the first month of bitcoin's existence.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:03.480
The financial and technical circles with regard to digital currencies stood in full swing at that time.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.080
Is this the satoshi of those 40 bitcoins?

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:12.000
There are theories that say that this bitcoin is being transferred from a wallet that is possibly attributed to Satoshi.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.640
Other theories disagree, because it does not fit the style of Batoshi and therefore it is often someone else; not Satoshi.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:22.360
Why are the movements of these inactive accounts causing such concern?

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:25.120
Because the world is anxious to know who this Satoshi is.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:31.200
There is no information about him, so tracking his currencies and their movement can give us any indication of his identity.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:38.240
In addition, he owns 5.7% of the total Bitcoin that exists all over the world so far.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:44.040
Thus, if he moves only a part of this portion; the whole cryptocurrency market will go upside down.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:48.480
This may be why his accounts remained inactive since 2009.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:54.280
This was a summary of Satoshi Nakamoto's huge net worth and the several attempts to track it to figure out who he is.

